class.description:
A [FlowGraphPlayback] runs a [FlowGraph]: it knows the current node, moves along flow connections, reads data inputs on demand, enters subgraphs, keeps variables in a [FlowBlackboard], and tells the game what happens through signals. Its state can be saved into a [FlowGraphState] and restored later (savegames). [FlowGraphPlayer] plays it in the scene tree; you can also use a playback on its own.
[b]Steps.[/b] [method start] enters the start node. Each entered node runs its [method FlowNode._enter]; a node that continues leaves through the flow output from [method FlowNode._choose_exit], and the playback enters the connected node in the same call. This repeats until a node waits ([constant ENTER_RESULT_WAIT]), the graph ends ([FlowEndNode] or a flow output without a connection), or the playback is stopped. Everything runs synchronously on the calling thread.
[b]Waiting.[/b] A waiting node holds the playback ([method is_waiting]). Continue with [method advance], [method advance_to_exit] or [method choose]. Nodes that wait for time implement [method FlowNode._process_waiting], driven by [method process]. To wait for a signal, connect the signal to [method advance] (one-shot) in [method FlowNode._enter].
[b]Data.[/b] Data nodes are not entered. When a node reads [method get_input_value], the playback evaluates the connected output ([method FlowNode._evaluate_output]) and, recursively, its inputs. An unconnected input reads the default value of its [FlowPort]; an unconnected input of a [FlowExpressionNode] reads the variable of the same name, if there is one.
[b]Subgraphs.[/b] A [FlowSubgraphNode] pushes its graph and starts it; when that graph ends, the playback returns to the subgraph node and continues through the output named like the end name (see [member FlowSubgraphNode.exit_names]) or [code]out[/code]. Node ids in signals and methods belong to the graph on top of the stack ([method get_current_graph]).
[b]Control from signal handlers.[/b] Signal handlers and nodes may call [method stop], [method start], [method advance] or [method travel]; the step that emitted the signal then returns without touching the state.
[b]Guards.[/b] [member max_steps_per_advance], [member max_data_depth] and [member max_subgraph_depth] stop endless loops and runaway recursion (the Web build has a small stack): the playback prints an error, stops and returns [constant ERR_CYCLIC_LINK].
[codeblocks]
[gdscript]
var playback := FlowGraphPlayback.new()
playback.node_waiting.connect(func(id, node): print("Waiting at ", node.get_title()))
playback.finished.connect(func(end_id): print("Done"))
playback.start(preload("res://quests/intro.tres"))
# Later, for example when the player presses a button:
if playback.is_waiting():
    playback.advance()
[/gdscript]
[csharp]
var playback = new FlowGraphPlayback();
playback.NodeWaiting += (id, node) => GD.Print("Waiting at ", node.GetTitle());
playback.Finished += endId => GD.Print("Done");
playback.Start(GD.Load<FlowGraph>("res://quests/intro.tres"));
// Later, for example when the player presses a button:
if (playback.IsWaiting())
{
    playback.Advance();
}
[/csharp]
[/codeblocks]
